const body = `<h1>3.1a — The Turret: Distance &amp; the Detection Radius</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Until now, the world has been passive: coins wait to be collected and spikes wait to be bumped.
    The Turret is your first adversary that <em>decides</em>. Before it can shoot, it must be aware.
    Today, you break enemy awareness into smaller sub-problems, read Unity's spatial functions,
    and build <code>TurretController.cs</code> from scratch.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Building artificial intelligence starts with breaking down awareness into smaller questions:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li><em>Who is my target and where are they?</em> (Target acquisition)</li>
        <li><em>How far away is the target right now?</em> (Distance measurement)</li>
        <li><em>Is the target inside my danger zone?</em> (Selection condition)</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Comparing Targeting &amp; Distance Functions</h3>
        <p><strong>Sub-problem 1: Locating the Target.</strong> Compare these two Unity techniques:</p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Example</th><th>Pros &amp; Cons</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>GameObject.FindWithTag</code></td><td><code>GameObject.FindWithTag("Player")</code></td><td>Searches the entire scene by tag. Very flexible, but scanning all scene objects is slow if called repeatedly in <code>Update()</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>[SerializeField]</code> reference</td><td><code>[SerializeField] private Transform playerTransform;</code></td><td>Directly linked in the Inspector. Zero search time, but requires manual wiring and fails if targets spawn dynamically at runtime.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p><strong>Sub-problem 2: Measuring Distance.</strong> Compare these two math approaches:</p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Function</th><th>Example</th><th>Performance Note</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Vector3.Distance</code></td><td><code>Vector3.Distance(posA, posB)</code></td><td>Calculates the exact Euclidean distance (\(\\sqrt{\Delta x^2 + \Delta y^2}\)). Clean and readable.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sqrMagnitude</code></td><td><code>(posB - posA).sqrMagnitude &lt;= range * range</code></td><td>Compares squared distances. Skips the square root operation entirely, saving massive CPU power when hundreds of enemies search at once!</td></tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
            <li>If you use <code>GameObject.FindWithTag</code>, why should it be called once in <code>Start()</code> and stored in a variable (cached), rather than called 60 times a second in <code>Update()</code>?</li>
            <li>In what scenarios would you choose <code>Vector3.Distance</code>, and in what scenarios is <code>sqrMagnitude</code> preferred?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Analyze the Architecture</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Examine how an automated security spotlight finds its target, measures Euclidean distance,
        and triggers an alert when an object enters its detection sphere:
    </p>

    <pre><code>using UnityEngine;

// Automated motion-sensing spotlight
public class ProximityLight : MonoBehaviour
{
    // Designer tuning knob for sensing radius
    [SerializeField] private float sensorRadius = 8.0f;

    // Cached reference to the target's transform
    private Transform targetTransform;

    private void Start()
    {
        // Cache the target reference ONCE at startup to avoid expensive per-frame searches
        GameObject detectedObj = GameObject.FindWithTag("TargetDrone");
        if (detectedObj != null)
        {
            targetTransform = detectedObj.transform;
        }
        else
        {
            Debug.LogWarning("ProximityLight: No target found with tag 'TargetDrone'!");
        }
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        // Safety guard: do nothing if no target exists
        if (targetTransform == null) return;

        // Measure straight-line 3D Euclidean distance
        float currentDist = Vector3.Distance(transform.position, targetTransform.position);

        // Evaluate proximity threshold
        if (currentDist &lt;= sensorRadius)
        {
            Debug.Log("Motion detected! Target distance: " + currentDist);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is calling <code>GameObject.FindWithTag()</code> in <code>Start()</code> considered a best practice, while calling it inside <code>Update()</code> causes major game lag?</li>
            <li>What does the guard clause <code>if (targetTransform == null) return;</code> do? What error would crash the console if this line were removed and no player existed?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>TurretController.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>File &amp; Class Name:</strong> In <code>Assets/Scripts/</code>, create a new script named <code>TurretController.cs</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Detection Range Knob:</strong> Declare a serialized private float field named <code>maxAttackRange</code> with a default value of <code>7f</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Cached Reference:</strong> Declare a private <code>Transform playerTransform;</code> field.</li>
            <li><strong>Initialization in Start:</strong> In <code>Start()</code>, search the scene for the player using <code>GameObject.FindWithTag("Player")</code>. If found, store its <code>transform</code> in <code>playerTransform</code>. If null, log an error alerting the developer that no GameObject has the tag "Player".</li>
            <li><strong>Distance Check in Update:</strong> In <code>Update()</code>:
                <ul>
                    <li>Guard against null player reference (return early if null).</li>
                    <li>Calculate the distance between the turret's position (<code>transform.position</code>) and the player's position (<code>playerTransform.position</code>) using <code>Vector3.Distance</code>.</li>
                    <li>If the distance is less than or equal to <code>maxAttackRange</code>, log a message showing that the player is in range along with the calculated distance.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — Attach to the Turret in Unity.</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>Open the <strong>Overworld</strong> scene and select the <strong>Turret</strong> GameObject.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Add Component</strong> and choose <strong>Turret Controller</strong>.</li>
        <li>Notice the <strong>Max Attack Range</strong> field in the Inspector defaults to <code>7</code>.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — Test the boundary live.</strong>
    Press Play. Walk your player toward the turret while watching the Console:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>At distance <code>7.1</code>: The Console is quiet.</li>
        <li>At distance <code>6.9</code>: The Console repeatedly logs <code>Player in range!</code>.</li>
        <li>Walk away past 7: The messages stop.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Testing Range Conditions</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you temporarily change the Player's tag to <code>Untagged</code> and press Play? What error message appears in the Console, and why?</li>
            <li>In <code>TurretController.cs</code>, what is the difference between <code>&lt;= maxAttackRange</code> and <code>&lt; maxAttackRange</code>? At what exact distance value would the behavior differ?</li>
            <li>In the Inspector, change <code>Max Attack Range</code> to <code>2</code>. Walk around the turret. How does changing this single knob transform the turret from a sniper into a close-quarters sentry?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Visualizing the Aggro Zone</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Add this Unity built-in function to the bottom of <code>TurretController.cs</code>:
